---
import { site } from '../data/site';

// Values transcribed from the genuine capture at docs/images/turboj-classic.png.
// A single cache hit and execution are evidence of this run, not a benchmark.
const capturedRun = `Message · CACHE HIT
0 errors
Cache-hit operation: 0.03 ms

FAST RUN · Hello

Hello TurboJ!

exit 0
Execution: 1.17 ms
Round trip: 2.22 ms
Completed`;
---

<section class="section performance-section" id="performance" aria-labelledby="performance-heading">
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="section-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">REAL CODE. REAL MEASUREMENTS.</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading" id="performance-heading">Fast is something you measure.</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="performance-layout">
      <div class="performance-copy">
        <p class="body-copy">TurboJ is being built around a simple engineering goal: make the time between changing Java code and seeing the result as short as possible.</p>
        <p class="body-copy">The current prototype already reports compiler activity and execution timings directly in the interface.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="performance-console" role="group" aria-labelledby="capture-heading">
        <h3 id="capture-heading">A captured prototype run</h3>
        <pre><code>{capturedRun}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="performance-note">
      <p class="body-copy">These measurements are from the <a href={site.links.original}>native Hello.java capture</a>, not the illustrated HelloWorld.java replay above.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">These values come from one captured prototype run. A cache hit is not a fresh compilation, and an individual execution is not a benchmark.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">We'll publish reproducible performance measurements as the project develops.</p>
      <p class="section-closing">No invented benchmarks. Just measured results.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
